{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the given problem, we start by recognizing that the polynomial \\(x^{98} + x^{97} + x^{96} + \\dots + x^2 + x + 1\\) is the 99th cyclotomic polynomial, which has roots that are the 99th roots of unity excluding 1. Let these roots be \\(r_1, r_2, \\dots, r_{98}\\). These roots satisfy \\(r_1^{99} = 1\\) and \\(r_i \\neq 1\\) for \\(i = 1, 2, \\dots, 98\\).\n\nWe need to find the value of the sum\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{r_k^2}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify the expression \\(\\frac{r_k^2}{r_k + 1}\\). Notice that since \\(r_k^{99} = 1\\), we have \\(r_k^{98} = \\frac{1}{r_k}\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the sum as:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{r_k^2}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this, we use the identity:\n\\[\n\\frac{r_k^2}{r_k + 1} = \\frac{r_k^2 + 1 - 1}{r_k + 1} = \\frac{r_k^2 + 1}{r_k + 1} - \\frac{1}{r_k + 1} = r_k + \\frac{1 - r_k}{r_k + 1} = r_k - 1 + \\frac{2}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum becomes:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\left( r_k - 1 + \\frac{2}{r_k + 1} \\right) = \\sum_{k=1}^{98} r_k - 98 + \\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{2}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the roots of the polynomial \\(x^{98} + x^{97} + \\dots + x + 1 = 0\\) is given by the negative of the coefficient of \\(x^{97}\\) divided by the leading coefficient, which is \\(-1\\). Therefore, \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} r_k = -1\\).\n\n","prefix_end":1252,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,2661,3491,11,582,1191,553,48083,429,279,47311,17767,87,47822,24,23,92,488,856,47822,24,22,92,488,856,47822,24,21,92,488,1124,67816,488,856,61,17,488,856,488,220,16,57758,374,279,220,24,24,339,31243,354,3075,47311,11,892,702,19703,429,525,279,220,24,24,339,19703,315,30326,43778,220,16,13,6771,1493,19703,387,17767,81,62,16,11,435,62,17,11,1124,67816,11,435,15159,24,23,11035,568,4220,19703,26553,17767,81,62,16,47822,24,24,92,284,220,16,5775